While I avidly await the implementation of the VonMises, I thought I
might try my hand at fitting a non-circular Gaussian+uniform
approximation to the original circular VonMises+uniform model I
described earlier. What I'm getting stuck with now is that I'd like
the parameters that determine individual performance (mean of the
Gaussian, sd of the Gaussian, mixing proportion) to be sampled across
individuals from a Gaussian multivariate distribution. While the
documentation describes how to use multi_normal at the level of
generating data from individuals, but not how to use it to specify the
sampling of parameter values for individuals, nor how to have a 3D
multivariate while ensuring that the sigma matrix is positive
definite. Any help in clarifying how to go about this would be greatly
appreciated! Here's the code I have so far, which simply implements
the sampling of the 3 parameters as from independent Gaussians:

data {
  int<lower=0> N;
  int<lower=0> T;
  real<lower=-pi(),upper=pi()> y[N,T];
}
parameters {
  real logitRho[N];
  real Mu[N];
  real logSigmaSq[N];

  real logitRho_mean;
  real<lower=-pi()/4,upper=pi()/4> Mu_mean;
  real logSigmaSq_mean;

  real<lower=0> logitRho_sd;
  real<lower=0> Mu_sd;
  real<lower=0> logSigmaSq_sd;
}
transformed parameters {
  real Rho[N];
  real Sigma[N];
  Rho[N] <- plogis(logitRho[N]);
  Sigma[N] <- sqrt(exp(logSigmaSq[N]));
}
model {
  logitRho ~ normal(logitRho_mean,logitRho_sd);
  Mu ~ normal(Mu_mean,Mu_sd);
  logSigmaSq ~ normal(logSigmaSq_mean,logSigmaSq_sd);
  for (n in 1:N){

	  for (t in 1:T) {
		lp__ <- lp__ +
			log_sum_exp(

				log(Rho) + normal_log(Mu,Sigma)
				, log(1-Rho) + uniform_log(Mu,-pi(),pi())
			);
	  }
}

