Hi Bob, and thanks again for your time !

As far as I know, the default in MCMCglmm is an adaptive Metropolis-Hastings algorithm. I'm not sure about mlwin (I don't use it very often and only dusted it off today to compare this model).

I am beginning to wonder if the models are equivalent. The stan model has resulted in a more output than I am used to seeing with MCMCglmm or other glmm packages (such as lme4).  Normally I expect a single variance estimate for the random intercept (school) and the residual plus an estimate for the overall mean (intercept). The intercept estimate from stan (beta) corresponds with the output I get with lme4/MCMCglmm, although the effective sample size for beta is only 3

.For the random intercept stan doesn't seem to produce the variance for it - instead I get 300 values u2[1] to u[300].  The effective samples for these seem quite good (1400-1500) but I don't understand what they are !

It also produces estimates of 0.0 for tau and tau_u2.

FWIW this is the output I get from lme4, which I trust because these data are artificial: I simulated them (I could send you the code if you like ?) so I know what estimates are reasonable:

Linear mixed model fit by REML
Formula: outcome ~ 1 + (1 | school.ID)
........
Random effects:
 Groups    Name        Variance Std.Dev.
 school.ID (Intercept)  34.616   5.8835
 Residual              105.751  10.2835
Number of obs: 30000, groups: school.ID, 100

Fixed effects:
            Estimate Std. Error t value
(Intercept)   20.669      0.591   34.97

Any further insight would be appreciated very much.

Thanks again !
JK

