On 10/8/12 3:28 PM, Richard McElreath wrote:
> On Monday, October 8, 2012 11:51:14 AM UTC-7, Bob Carpenter wrote:
>
>     You can split each variable into observed and missing
>     components, put everything back together in a transformed
>     param, and you're good to go (though it's not the most
>     efficient approach imaginable).
>
>
> I was playing with this approach over the weekend, benchmarking against the data splitting approach shown in the manual.
> Merging the imputed values into the right places in the mixed vector presents some obstacles. I found a quick solution,
> but I'm hoping there's a better algorithm in the transformed parameters that Bob and the other real programmers might
> suggest.
>
> First, here are my parameters and transformed parameters blocks:

If you can't sort the x so that the missing values
are ar the end, you also need an x_non_missidx[i].

>      parameters {
>          real<lower=0> sigma;
>          real alpha;
>          real beta;
>          real x_miss[N_miss];        // imputed x values
>          real x_mu;                  // mean of dist of x values
>          real<lower=0> x_sigma;      // sd of dist of x values
>      }
>      transformed parameters {
>          real x_impute[N];
>          for ( i in 1:N ) {
>              x_impute[i] <- x[i];
>              for ( g in 1:(x_is_miss[i]) ) {
>                  x_impute[i] <- x_miss[ x_missidx[i] ];
>              }
>          }
>      }
> |

> The above approach works, producing the same inferences as the data splitting approach, but does run a bit slower. I
> used simulated data of 1000 observed y's and 800 observed x's in a simple linear regression (so imputed 200 values).
> Call the approach above the "vector merging" approach. 10k samples (1k warmup) took:
>
>     user  system elapsed
>   61.975   1.107  62.967
>
> The data splitting approach took instead:
>
>     user  system elapsed
>   57.367   1.091  58.140

That's not as much of a penalty as I expected.
Of course, it'll depend on the missingness model and
whether you can apply that just to the missing data.
If so, it shouldn't be too bad for speed.

> I'll attach the entire script that defines both versions of the model, in case it's helpful to others.

Thanks.  As you can see, we're working out programming
patterns for Stan as we go.  Some of them we anticipated,
but users have already had a number of reasonable requests
we never anticipated.

