I agree with everything Bob said. And would add that if you want to do
sampling for LDA, it's likely that (for now) you'll be better off with
the collapsed Gibbs sampler of Griffiths and Steyver (2004). There's
an implementation in Jonathan Chang's lda R package, which for some
reason was removed from CRAN but can still be downloaded here:
http://cran.r-project.org/src/contrib/Archive/lda/

Matt

On Mon, Oct 8, 2012 at 1:51 PM, Bob Carpenter <ca...@alias-i.com> wrote:
> We don't really recommend Stan for models like LDA.
> The problem is that the multi-modality of the posterior
> makes Bayesian inference intractable, not just for Stan,
> but for any known technique.
>
> That being the case, you might as well go for an optimizer.
> Like the very scalable online one Matt (the inventor of
> our sampler, NUTS) wrote for Vowpal Wabbit:
>
>   http://hunch.net/~vw/
>
> and described in this paper:
>
>   http://www.cs.princeton.edu/~blei/papers/HoffmanBleiBach2010b.pdf
>
> The reason Stan's consuming so much memory is that we're
> building up the log probability expression tree in memory to
> use to compute auto-diff.
>
> I believe PyMC uses Metropolis, which may run and provide
> samples, but isn't going to give you very efficient sampling,
> even around a single mode.
>
> Finally, the Stan model isn't well formed because
> we don't do integer sampling.  So the "topic ~ categorical(...)"
> isn't going to do what you think -- what it will do is
> add log categorical(topic|theta[i]) to the log probabilty
> where topic is uninitialized.
>
> What you need to do is marginalize out the discrete
> variables because Stan doesn't sample them.  Here's
> a note on how to do that for basic LDA:
>
> http://lingpipe-blog.com/2011/04/14/summing-out-topic-assignments-ld/
>
> This can also be a useful move if you're applying some
> kind of optimization.
>
>
>> model {
>> int start;
>> int topic;
>> for (n in 1:K) {
>> psi[n] ~ dirichlet(beta[n]);
>> }
>> for (n in 1:D) {
>> theta[n] ~ dirichlet(alpha);
>> }
>> start <- 1;
>> for (i in 1:D) {
>>   for (j in start:doc_ind[i] - 1) {
>>     topic ~ categorical(theta[i]);
>>     words[j] ~ categorical(psi[topic + 1]);
>>   }
>>   start <- doc_ind[i];
>> }
>> }
>
> Also, I don't follow the use of start here.  Why not
> write this loop as in the Blei et al. LDA paper?  That is,
> as:
>
> for (d in 1:D) {
>   theta[d] ~ dirichlet(alpha);
>   for (j in 1:N[d]) {
>     topic[j] ~ categorical(theta[d]);
>     words[j] ~ categorical(psi[topic[j]]);
>   }
> }
>
> The indexing in Stan is from 1, by the way, so I doubt
> you wanted the +1 inside of psi.
>
> You'd also need an index, as in topic[j], if we
> supported discrete sampling.  At least the above is how
> I'd write it in BUGS/JAGS (modulo their matrix slicing
> syntax which I can never remember).
>
> - Bob
>
>
> On 10/8/12 3:52 PM, Julian Brooke wrote:
>>
>> Hello folks,
>>
>> I'm trying to test some adaptions of the latent Dirichlet allocation (LDA)
>> model, adaptions where collapsed Gibbs
>> sampling isn't going to work right out of the box; I thought I'd try using
>> a general purpose sampler, and STAN looked
>> like it might be the best option in terms of speed. To begin, I coded up a
>> version of standard LDA in stan (see below)
>> and it seems to be working in very small data sets. However, if I push the
>> number of text/size of the vocabulary to
>> anything approaching what I'd need for a real world application, STAN
>> quickly uses up all the memory on my server (92
>> Gigs!) and crashes, before the sampling processes even begins. I can scale
>> it down a bit to the point where it has
>> enough memory to start sampling, but I started a 50 text corpus a few days
>> ago and its still running. This is much worse
>> than the other general-purpose sampler I've tried (pymc). Anyway, I
>> thought I'd check to see if maybe the problem was in
>> my code, otherwise I have to reluctantly conclude that STAN (and
>> general-purpose sampling in general) is not what I
>> need, and I might as well go back to trying to hack existing LDA
>> implementations for my purposes, which is not ideal:(.
>> If anyone has any advice, specific or general, it would be much
>> appreciated.
>>
>> (Note: I don't sample the hyperparameters, instead I pass them in. Beta is
>> a matrix of vectors so that I can use it to
>> seed topics with specific words; Also, the entire corpus is a single
>> vector, with switches between documents stored in a
>> separate matrix (doc_ind); oh, and I have to add 1 to topic because
>> categorical gives a range starting with zero whereas
>> arrays start with 1, this mismatch seems like a bad design choice BTW)
>>
>> data {
>>   int<lower=1> K; //topics
>>   int<lower=1> V; //vocabulary
>>   int<lower=1> D; //number of documents
>>   int<lower=1> T; // total number of words
>>   vector[K] alpha;
>>   vector[V] beta[K];
>>   int words[T];
>>   int doc_ind[D];
>> }
>>
>> parameters {
>> simplex[V] psi[K];
>> simplex[K] theta[D];
>> }
>>
>> model {
>> int start;
>> int topic;
>> for (n in 1:K) {
>> psi[n] ~ dirichlet(beta[n]);
>> }
>> for (n in 1:D) {
>> theta[n] ~ dirichlet(alpha);
>> }
>> start <- 1;
>> for (i in 1:D) {
>> for (j in start:doc_ind[i] - 1) {
>> topic ~ categorical(theta[i]);
>> words[j] ~ categorical(psi[topic + 1]);
>> }
>> start <- doc_ind[i];
>> }
>> }
>>
>> --
>>
>>
>

> --
>
