On 10/18/12 2:22 PM, Josh Korman wrote:
...
> I have included a simplified example that gets at my multiple
> definition problem. Here I want to estimate abundance from two
> population (N[]) and assume that each of these estimates comes from a
> hyper distribution with a fixed value for tau. The objective is to
> estimate the overall abundance
> (SuperN) as an information weighted average of the two estimates
> conditional on the fixed value of tau. This works fine using the
> uncommented lines
> (expu<- and u~dpois) below. Problem is that in my more complex model,
> N is a function of other estimated parameters so need to do something
> like the two commented-out lines, which won't work in WinBugs because
> N is defined twice (as a logical expression and as a stochastic node).
>
> As for as you second point, you say that STAN cannot estimate discrete
> parameters. Would N from u~dbin(theta, N) be estimable if you allow N
> to be continuous rather than discrete? Or is the order of a binomial
> (e.g. N in this example) just not estimable in STAN?

The function signatures for the distributions are in the manual --
binomial(N,theta) requires N to be an integer.
(We're not using the R notation dbin() for pmfs and pdfs).

Unfortunately, the discretizing transforms like floor() lose derivative
information because they're flat step functions.  So while we allow them as
transforms, they don't play into the log probability (we should make that
clearer in the manual).

Stan also uses different parameterizations than BUGS for some distros (e.g.,
deviation rather than precision for normals).

More below.

> Example CODE
>
> r, M, and u are data, p,N, and superN are parameters to be estimated.
>
> model
> {
> 	mu~dnorm(0,1.0E-6)		#hyper prior on log of single
> escapement estimate
>
> 	for (i in 1:2)
> 	{
> 		p[i]~dbeta(1,1)		#uninformative prior n p
> 		r[i]~dbin(p[i],M[i])	#binomial likelihood to estimate
> capture probability p given recaps (r) and marks release (M)
>
> 		logN[i]~dnorm(mu,tau)	#log N for each population comes
> from a common (super) distribution)
> 		N[i]<-exp(logN[i])		#transform
>
> 		#The following two lines work as expected
> 		expu[i]<-N[i]*p[i]	#expected number of unmarked fish
> captured on 2nd pass given N and p
> 		u[i]~dpois(expu[i])	#This makes N[i] a stochastic node
> which allows HBM to work
>
> 		#Replacing the previous 2 lines with the following 2 does
not work
> due to multiple definitions of node N[]
> 		#u[i]~dbin(p[i],N[i])
> 		#N[i]<-u[i]/p[i]

I don't understand what you'd like this to do.

Stan is imperative, so what would happen in Stan is that you'd define N[i]
the second time and never use it.

If you just want to sample u[i] twice because its probability is a product,
that's OK in Stan:

    u[i] ~ poisson(exp(logN[i]) * p[i]);

    u[i] ~ binomial(floor(exp(logN[i])), p[i]);

That'll result in the following effect on the probability function being
defined:

   p(u,...) propto poisson(u[i]|...) * binomial(u[i]|...)

What's the log probability function supposed to be?

Then if you want to look at something, just define a new generated quantity
for it.

- Bob

