> Is this what you meant by "You will then run into a problem where your year variable is a factor rather than a numeric variable" ? I didn't know how to specify a factor in the data{} section : can you tell me how to do that ?

That is what I meant. You can't specify a factor in the data{} section of a .stan file. You would need to convert the factor inside R to integer codes. So, if for example, you had a factor in R like

data(InsectSprays)
summary(InsectSprays$spray)

You would need to coerce it to an integer vector like

int_spray <- as.integer(InsectSprays$spray)
summary(int_spray)

> Then it compiled and seemed to be running, until I got the popup:
>
> Microsoft Visual C++ Runtime Library
>
> This application has requested the Rutime to terminate in an unusual way.
> Please contact the application's support team for more information.
>
> And the message that R had stopped working, to which I answered "close", though it didn't close down and the process seemed to be still in a loop (thrashing the CPU but unresponsive) so I had to kill the process.
>
> Please advise

According to the Stan documentation, the MSVC compiler can't compile Stan models, which your experience seems to be inconsistent with. In R, can you tell us the output of

system("R CMD config CXX")

Hopefully, someone who uses Windows will know more than I do about this.

Ben

