That makes sense because the point of the Matt trick is to use standard normal priors wherever possible, because they lead to the most efficient sampling. Putting a gamma(0,0) prior on v2_z is just wrong as far as I can tell.

I managed to get it to run 32 chains each for 2000 iterations (on Linux) without getting the runtime error you got before. It didn't converge, but this example seems to have the same problem as the example from Shige: The default initial values are very far from the truth. Standardizing y or providing reasonable initial values should be sufficient.

Ben

