On 10/21/12 4:38 PM, Joe King wrote:
> Dear Bob
>
> Please accept my apologies for wrong use of terminology. I only began learning statistics in the last few months and I
> find much of the terminology very confusing. I learned about multilevel (or is it "hierarchical" ?)  models from my
> maths teacher in June and the method I learned was to first fit a "null model" with no covariates, so I am sorry for
> calling this a "random slope" model - but it will be after I add the covariates in the next stage I think.

They're called "hierarchical" when the effects are
all nested inside each other, such as students
within classrooms within school districts within
states.

They're called "multilevel" when the effects are not
nested.  Years and schools are cross-cutting categories,
so a model with both is multilevel, not hierarchical.

As to "slope" and "intercept", a typical univariate linear
regression of y on x would be:

for (n in 1:N)
  y[n] ~ normal(alpha * x[n] + beta, sigma);

where alpha is the "slope" and beta the "intercept" of the
line defining the location of the normal distribution generating
y[n] based on x[n].

> Thank you for helping me ! I appreciate it very much !

Sorry the program ran into a bug with our syntax right away!
Thanks for letting us know, because I was able to fix it
for the next release.

I love the idea of starting learning stats with
multilevel models in Stan!  There's a section in the
user's guide, but for more info, you should really
read Gelman and Hill's 2009 regression book.  We
more or less built Stan to deal with extending
the gnarly models in their book to even more complex
structures.

- Bob

