On Tuesday, October 23, 2012 5:08:11 PM UTC-4, Andrew Gelman wrote:
> Hmm, now I'm confused.  Here's what I think you're saying.  Consider 3 possibilities:

or 4 :). My original FAQ email was a little off on this part. Read the edited version at

https://groups.google.com/d/msg/stan-users/4gv3fNCqSNk/VonPUkdcZuUJ

and tell me whether it makes sense. But my understanding of your 4 possibilities are ...

> 1.
> real theta
> theta ~ normal (mu,sigma)

fine, as expected

> 2.
> real<lower=0,upper=0.5> theta
> theta ~ normal (mu, sigma)

will yield the wrong results if mu or sigma were unknown

> 3.
> real<lower=0,upper=0.5> theta
> theta ~ normal (mu, sigma) T[0,0.5]

will yield the right results, but truncation is not implemented for all distributions yet

> 4.
> real theta
> theta ~ normal (mu, sigma) T[0,0.5]

should be avoided because it generates proposals for theta with the log-posterior is not defined or is negative infinity.

Ben

